y actually fall into these five  categories  segmentation isolation identity access  management data security supply chain  application security  now we don't have time to drive into all  these 20 in this 25 minutes talk but we  will talk about how anthos can help  with several of these requirements right  out of the box as you can see the same  five requirements  we've later done we put them down into  five pillars and out of the box  anthous offers a lot of security  capabilities  each control aligns with the common  compliance requirements that we talked  about in the previous slide whether it's  going to be segmentation networking  iam data security and encryption secure  supply chain and application security  so as you can see most of these features  to help you achieve your security  posture  but as i mentioned before it's the  shared responsibility model  and you need to know how to blend the  controls together appropriately and  understand what you are responsible for  for that what we're going to be  introducing is anthony's blueprints  and to talk in detail about that i'd  like to call back and  and cool thanks harish  so before we dive into the anthos  blueprints first let's talk about our  security blueprint so we've designed  security blueprints to help some of this  complexity that uh harish described  to simplify it so the first security  blueprint we have is called  security posture so this is a collection  of resources and code that  deliver granular and specific security  postures to a workload such as maybe a  customer-facing application or back-end  process  for example securing data or securing  api endpoints  the next one that we offer is called a  landing zone so this is a pre-configured  environment with a standard set of  secure cloud infrastructure  policy and guidelines so this can be  something that's otherwise very  complicated to set up it might take  months of investigation  implementation by trial and error to get  right the first time so an example this  is pci for anthos  regardless of the type of security  blueprint they're all designed to be  easy to use and deploy  and we also employ all of google cloud  security best practices in the blueprint  so you can say they're secure  by default so one of the first security  landing zones that we we released was  pci  on gke and coming soon is pci on anthos  so these help customers get started  with compliance on anthos quicker so it  also allows you to quickly and easily  deploy workloads on anthos  that align to pci dss and repeatable  supported and secure ways so let's look  at everything that's included in the  blueprint  so the pci and anthony's blueprint  contains many opinionated reference  architecture diagrams  depicting the infrastructure and  application deployments while they're  intended to be used with deployable  assets  they actually show you how to create  diagrams of your containerized card  holder data environments  and the boundaries between pci and  non-pci workloads  which is a requirement in pci audits the  blueprint also contains different  deployable assets  and then we also include pci dss 3.2.1  mappings to the solutions  and a compliance white paper  so with our anthos on gcp blueprint we  provide you with terraform templates to  set up your  infrastructure these include org  policies projects vpcs and gke clusters  and as harish described about the shift  in the shared responsibility model  our on-prem we only provide you with  reference architectures for squares one  and two  but with both of the blueprints we  provide you with service mesh profiles  gke configurations  acm policies and then a demo app to test  out the environment  configuration and setup  so let's pivot to segmentation there are  two types of segmentation  so the first one is macro segmentation  so these are things that you do inside  or outside the cluster so you segment  the users  networks and applications using  different google cloud  features and tools this really helps you  set aside the scope  of the audit and then we have micro  segmentation so these are things that  you do inside the cluster so let's  double click on that a little bit  so with gke micro segmentation we offer  many ways to do this so we have network  policies this this helps with layer 3  and 4  network level segmentation then we have  gke name spaces for virtual clusters to  segment teams or maybe app versus  prod or app dev versus prod then we have  gke  private clusters so you have clusters  with private ip space and then antho  service mesh  but the net net here is there are many  ways to do micro segmentation in a  cluster  so for the pci on anthos on-prem  blueprint the first layer of  segmentation is addressed through  separate clusters  so we create two clusters two user  clusters one for in scope  or for your card holder data cluster and  then one for the out of scope workloads  and then building upon the segmentation  we have segmented out of the pods within  a cluster but  you further need to demonstrate  separation of maybe dev and test versus  prod environments to your auditor  or you need to segment application  profiles so this is where you would use  different name spaces  and then oftentimes you're presented  with a requirement to protect the  routing information and the private ip  spaces  this is where you mark a cluster as  private in such a cluster  and the master become inaccessible from  the public internet  and then the nodes in the private  cluster do not have public ip  addresses and so the admin and user  clusters are private for the on-prem  blueprint and the gcp blueprint  so looking at some of the reference  architectures that are provided as part  of this blueprint  you can see here that again the end  scope and out of scope cluster  separation and then we have antho  service mesh so it's a single mesh for  multi-clusters and then our demo app is  called online boutique so this  is an e-commerce micro services demo  application so this allows you to test  out the controls in the architecture  the pci dss audit burden is minimized by  moving the microservices  from that demo application that don't  touch payment card  data into the out of scope cluster this  reference architecture  details this layout this also helps you  with your own architectures and  documentation  and then looking more specifically at  pci requirements so pci requirement 4  talks about  encrypting data in flight specifically  of card holder data across open and  public networks  for our on-prem blueprint the in-scope  and out-of-scope clusters are in  separate networks which helps with  strong isolation for pci boundaries  this also means that the cross-cluster  communication must go through an istio  gateway  all cross customer communication uses  mutual tls  authentication and encryption citadel is  also included for managing the internal  certificates  so a lot of the blueprint is for day  zero but we also want to talk about  day one and day two sort of scenarios so  this is where policies code or shifting  left  happens so you wanna you know setting up  initially is great  everything's pci compliant but as you're  deploying new containers or new code  into your environment how do you make  sure  these um meet pci requirements so to  help with this  we have binary authorization anthos  config management  and anthos policy controller so let's  look at these in a little bit more  detail  so for a requirement such as establish a  process to identify security  vulnerabilities  you can use container registry  vulnerability scanning  so this checks the images that you push  into the registry for known security  vulnerabilities  it is constantly refreshing its  vulnerability database by ingesting  corresponding vendor security feeds  so that you can be notified of new  vulnerabilities that may be a  affected scan containers you can also  tie this in with pub sub to get  notifications  so you can integrate this in with your  ci cd process or file  bugs for newly discovered  vulnerabilities  and another requirement is in to ensure  all systems and software are protected  from known vulnerabilities this is where  binary authorization can help  so it ensures that only properly signed  containers are deployed into production  or other environments  you can use binary authorization to  create deployment policies based on  container image metadata  so these policies can be based on  attestation such as vulnerability scan  or other types of signatures such as  maybe being built by a particular  pipeline or having gone through a  specific qa process  again this ensures that only trusted  images are deployed into your  environment  so let's put all this together so when  code is checked into github or your  source code repository it triggers a  build in cloud build  cloud build then generates a new  container image it  will create a build record of the  contain that contains information on the  image including the location  the source code from which the image was  built  and then that triggers a vulnerability  scan so the vulnerability scan is done  the results of that scan are stored as  part of the metadata for that container  and as mentioned on the previous slide  you can configure binary authorization  to require attestations based on the  vulnerability scan results  so this prevents images with known  vulnerabilities beyond a certain level  so maybe say hi  from being deployed you can also have  other requirements like only allowing  images from trusted sources  so if someone tries to deploy one of  these unauthorized images binary  authorization will stop it  and all of this is logged in cloud audit  log  so now anthony's config management or  acm  so with acm you can create common  configurations across  all your infrastructure including custom  policies and apply it to both on-prem  and across clouds anthos config  management evaluates to changes and  rolls them out to all the kubernetes  clusters so that your desired state is  always reflected  and so based on open policy agents  gateway acm policy controller provides  first-class integration between  opa and kubernetes via custom controller  so this turns rego policies into  kubernetes objects allowing them to be  customized and deployed using your  standard workflows  when policy controller is configured  incoming objects can be  denied emission into your cluster or  audited to see if they violate  governance policies  so we offer quite a few sample policies  that you can use with acm they include  policies like requiring services to  disable  unauthorized access or label enforcement  but more specifically with our anthos  pci blueprints we include policies to  help enforce tls and again  this helps enforce pci requirement for  which talks about  encrypting data in transit specifically  of card holder data  across open and public networks  so as you can see from this slide  looking at some other  common controls we can see how services  like binary authorization  or policy controller and anthos config  management can help  and then lastly we tie all of this  together by mapping the various  components and policies  in the blueprint to pci dss 3.2.1  and we also include a pci white paper so  the white paper provides an independent  third-party assessment of the blueprint  performed by pci auditors  this not only gives you our customers  more confidence in our solution  it is also something that you can  provide your qsa as part of your audit  which hopefully will help speed up your  assessment  so to learn more about this um or our  other blueprint schedule a session with  your  google team or check out the links here  so there's one for  google cloud and pci and one for our  security blueprints  thank you

